.TH WHITEHTTPD 1 "2026-10-27" "whitehttpd 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whitehttpd \- a static file HTTP server that fits in one memory page
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whitehttpd
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-\-daemon ]
.RI [ wwwroot ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whitehttpd
serves files from
.I wwwroot
over HTTP. It is written in MIPS32 assembly and links against nothing: the
whole server is a 4092 byte static binary that occupies a single 4096 byte
memory page, so it loads in one page fault and never touches the heap.
.PP
The server speaks HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1, keeps connections alive, and
handles pipelined requests. It refuses to follow symbolic links and rejects
path traversal, so files outside the document root cannot be read.
.PP
There is no help flag. This page is the documentation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
Listen on
.IR port .
Default 8080. The socket is dual-stack: a single
.B AF_INET6
listener with
.B IPV6_V6ONLY
cleared accepts both IPv4 and IPv6 connections.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Detach from the terminal after binding. The process forks once, the parent
exits, and the child continues in the background.
.TP
.I wwwroot
Document root. The server
.BR chdir (2)s
into it at start. Default is the current directory. Any argument that starts
with a hyphen and is not one of the options above is rejected with exit status 1.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.TP
.B Methods
.B GET
and
.B HEAD.
Anything else, and any malformed request line, gets
.BR "501 Not Implemented" .
.TP
.B Index
A request for a directory that contains
.B index.html
serves that file. A directory without it answers
.BR "403 Forbidden" ;
directory listings are not generated.
.TP
.B Redirect
A request for a directory without a trailing slash answers
.BR "301 Moved Permanently" ,
with the raw request path plus a slash in
.BR Location .
Percent escapes are passed through unchanged.
.TP
.B Range
A single
.B Range
header is honoured and answered with
.BR "206 Partial Content" .
Multiple ranges are ignored and the full body is returned, which
RFC 7233 allows.
.BR 416
is returned when the first byte position is past the end of the file.
.TP
.B Content types
Recognised by extension. The map is fixed, compiled in, 17 suffixes in three
families:
.RS
.IP text/ 4
html, htm, css, js, mjs, json, txt, md
.IP application/ 4
pdf, wasm, zip
.IP image/ 4
png, jpg, jpeg, gif, svg, ico
.RE
.IP
.B charset=utf-8
is appended to everything under
.BR text/ ,
and also to
.BR js ,
.B mjs
and
.BR json ,
which are labelled
.B application/javascript
and
.B application/json
but are text as far as a charset is concerned. Any other extension gets
.BR application/octet\-stream .
.TP
.B Headers
Every response carries
.BR "Server: whitehttpd" ,
.BR Connection
and
.BR "Content\-Length" .
There is no
.BR Date ,
no
.BR ETag ,
and no
.BR Last\-Modified ,
so a client cannot tell a fresh copy from a stale one except by asking again.
Cacheable responses carry
.BR "Cache\-Control: max\-age=60" .
Successful file responses carry
.BR "Accept\-Ranges: bytes" .
.TP
.B Limits
The request header must fit in 8192 bytes; anything longer is answered with
.BR "400 Bad Request" .
The URL part is capped at 1024 bytes. A connection that stays silent for
3 seconds is closed. Transfers run under a deadline so a stalled peer cannot
hold a worker forever.
.TP
.B Security
Symbolic links are refused at every path component, not just the last one,
so a link to an intermediate directory cannot escape the root. Both
.B /..
and encoded traversal are rejected with
.BR "403 Forbidden" .
A header line that begins with whitespace, or contains a bare CR, is rejected
with
.BR "400 Bad Request" .
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored at startup, through
.BR rt_sigaction (2).
Without this the server would die the first time a client closes its end of
the connection while a response is still being written, because every write to
a closed socket raises the signal and its default action kills the process.
.PP
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
are not handled. Use those to stop the server.
.SH FILES
None. Configuration is the command line alone.
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve the current directory on port 8080:
.PP
.RS
.nf
whitehttpd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Serve
.I /www
on port 80 as a background daemon:
.PP
.RS
.nf
whitehttpd --port 80 --daemon /www
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check that it answers, over IPv4 and over IPv6:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf 'GET / HTTP/1.0\\r\\nHost: x\\r\\n\\r\\n' | nc 127.0.0.1 8080
printf 'GET / HTTP/1.0\\r\\nHost: x\\r\\n\\r\\n' | nc ::1 8080
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
No TLS, no directory listing, no authentication, no chroot or privilege drop,
no dotfile filtering, no access log, no ETag or Last-Modified, no CGI, no
compression, no HTTP/2, no configuration file, no
.B \-\-addr
to bind a specific address. This is a server for public static files on a
trusted host in a directory with no secrets in it. It is not a replacement for
nginx or Apache.
.SH EXIT STATUS
1 on a usage error, or when any of
.BR socket (2),
.BR bind (2),
.BR chdir (2),
.BR listen (2)
fails. Otherwise the process runs until it is killed; it installs no exit path
of its own.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR httpd (8),
.BR nginx (8)
